   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises and their support systems to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Ministry of Economic Development of Belize, through its Rural Resilient Belize Program has implemented with the support of social partners and value chain actors (BELTRAIDE, BCCI, Ministry of Agriculture, National Trade Union Congress of Belize) a training program with their final beneficiaries on a national level to promote entrepreneurship and SME development. ILO, through the Joint SDG Fund Programme: Building a resilient Belize through universal, adaptive, sustainable social protection, together with the Ministry of Economic Development, Resilient Rural Belize Program, social partners and value chain actors (Belize Trade and Investment Development Service (BELTRAIDE), Belize Chamber of Commerce and Industry (BCCI), Ministry of Agriculture, Cooperatives Department, National Trade Union Congress of Belize (NTUCB)) has trained 21 local trainers in its program Gender and Entrepreneurship Together (GET Ahead) to strengthen their capacity to support entrepreneurship in rural areas. GET Ahead, a gender-sensitive entrepreneurship training programme was deployed in Belize,3 July 2023. This programme provides women and men with business management and essential soft skills they need for their venture. 90 women entrepreneurs, SMEs have been reached of which 28 in Red Bank Village in Stann Creek District from 7-11 August 2023, 25, Belize City, Belize District from 14-18 August 2023, 20,San Antonio and Santa Familia Villages, Cayo District, 21-25 August 2023; 20 San Pedro Village,Corozal District. The ILO supported the Belizean government to guarantee the operationalization of this program within current government initiatives as the Rural Resilient Belize Program and BELTRAIDE. With this approach, local economic development and livelihoods in targeted municipalities/local government bodies are strengthened through a gender and youth sensitive value chain development approach to employment and decent work.</narrative>
   </description>
