   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.2. Increased capacity of Member States to improve governance and sustainability of social protection systems</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The country, particularly through the Ministry of Sustainable Development, Climate Change and Disaster Risk Management and Ministry of Economic Development, the Social Security Board (SSB) and the Statistical Institute of Belize, has improved the statistical system on social protection. This has included contributions to the mapping of the data ecosystem mapping and reporting mechanism for the SDGs (and other international commitments and to support the monitoring exercise of #Plan Belize Medium Term Development Strategy (national development strategy). This mechanism aims to mainstream SDGs and guarantee they are aligned to national priorities. 
This result also contributes to the efforts of the Belize National Statistical System to have a platform for compiling data in one central location and via the Data portals | Statistical Institute of Belize (sib.org.bz). ILO has been providing technical support to reinforce and improve the statistical system, particularly data collection on social protection in Belize through: Desk review for the Social Security Inquiry (SSI); pre-validation workshop, July 2023, (attended by 70 representatives from government (more than 10 Ministries)), Central Bank, Statistical Institute of Belize, Social partners, 7 UN Agencies and NGOs, college and media; a 2nd workshop to validate the final data for the SSI in October 2023;  close collaboration with the Ministry of Sustainable Development, Climate Change and Disaster Risk Management, to contribute to the Voluntary National Report on the SDG&apos;s that the country is preparing to submit in 2024 and to guarantee continuity on the measurement of SDG indicator 1.3.1; 
collection of different information/ data to support the preparation of the actuarial study on pensions in Belize with the Social Security Board (SSB) employing the new ILO/PENSIONS model for the first time in the Caribbean. The process includes a set of specific training with the SSB on the use of the ILO/PENSIONS model (in July 2023). This training is allowing the SSB to be better prepared to understand and introduce adjustments for the different social protection benefits offered by the SSB. The SSB is preparing a pensions&apos; reform to be discussed by the end of 2023 and intends to use the actuarial valuation recommendations as well as some elements taken from the ILO/PENSIONS tool. 
Together with the data collection exercise for the actuarial valuation there was also a specific data collection exercise for the unemployment cost assessment. This assessment was presented to the Board of the SSB. (incl. workers and employers). The focus of collaboration on data collection during the biennium has enabled to reinforce national capacities for social protection data collection and production, particularly at the SSB level.</narrative>
   </description>
